    "created_at": "2016-06-02T04:55:50Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-06-02T05:12:27Z",
    "body": "I have thought about edge cases a bit more. Whenever custom element state is modified outside of the element queue it can lead to inconsistency. For example, if adoption resets custom element state, we get inconsistency like this:\n1. Enqueue an upgrade reaction for an element\n2. Before that reaction runs, move the element to a new document and insert it, queuing an upgrade reaction\n3. The first (old) upgrade step runs\n4. The second (up to date) upgrade step aborts&mdash;the element is left in the new document, using its definition, but with an old prototype.\n\nMaybe the guiding principle is that custom element state can be initialized when an element is created, but after that should be modified by reactions. (Upgrade and the HTMLElement constructor are in good shape.)\n\nAdoption could enqueue a 'downgrade' reaction. I think if this is _enqueued_ before any other reactions, then the element will end up with a consistent alignment of reaction callback and prototype.\n\nThese kind of issues sound a bit scary but it is really worthwhile sorting them out. \"Old\" custom elements punted on adoption and I think that was a mistake.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
